I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied tungstate framework notes first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best model through a GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep phase identification and quantitative fractions tied to the submitted project/profile so the outputs are reproducible.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type MnWO₄, but the 900 °C, one-hour/no-regrind synthesis can retain Mn₃O₄ or WO₃ and can support manganese-rich tungstates. I’m now separating the observed peak families and checking the installed reference-search/indexing/refinement interfaces before committing to a phase count.

The strong lines and their systematic absences match monoclinic P2/c MnWO₄; the diagnostic strongest lines of Mn₃WO₆ (20.49°, 28.75°, 35.24°), hausmannite (32.33°), and WO₃ (23.16–24.34° triplet) are absent at meaningful intensity. I’m proceeding with a one-phase MnWO₄ model, while I’ll use the refined residual—not chemistry alone—to set an upper bound on any missed phase.

The roughness correction is decisive: it lowers Rwp from 8.15% to 3.93%, only modestly above the 3.46% counting-statistics minimum reported by GSAS-II. The remaining residual is structured mainly within overlapped high-angle MnWO₄ reflections, so I’m testing the symmetry-allowed coordinate perturbations and directional broadening that the measurement model permits; I’m also constraining the final roughness/profile parameters back to physically admissible values.

A one-phase model remains the defensible inventory. Adding Mn₃WO₆ or WO₃ drives their scales to zero; a forced hausmannite term refines to only ~0.35(14) wt% and does not improve the stable global fit, so it is below a reliable detection/quantification threshold and will not be reported as a phase. I’m retaining “100% of modeled crystalline material” for MnWO₄ and will state the roughly 0.5 wt% trace-phase sensitivity and inability to quantify amorphous content.

The data support a single crystalline wolframite phase, MnWO₄ (P2/c). I’m finishing the final physically constrained refinement and then will package and cross-check the GSAS-II project, CIF, profile, JSON, and uncertainty report.

The refinement sweep is complete. The best stable one-phase model gives \(R_\mathrm{wp}\approx3.75\%\); alternate texture directions, displacement parameters, and extra-phase trials did not improve the fit credibly. I’m now auditing that selected project for convergence and physically sensible geometry before exporting it unchanged.

A final trace-phase check is warranted because the nominal 100 wt% is normalized only over modeled crystalline material. I’m testing unreacted Mn₃O₄/WO₃ and plausible Mn–O/Mn–W–O byproducts against the fixed final profile; this isolates whether any extra phase has statistically supported intensity rather than letting it absorb background flexibility.

The trace tests show only sub-noise trial scales (about 0–0.24 wt%) with no independently resolved diagnostic peaks, so they are not reliable phase identifications. Before export I’m removing one redundant geometric degree of freedom: zero shift and specimen displacement are >99.9% correlated, and fixing one conventionally should preserve the phase result while making the final project better conditioned.

The conditioning test confirms that both the constant zero term and specimen displacement are needed to reproduce the peak positions over the full angular range; forcing either to zero worsens \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) from 3.75% to at least 3.79%. I’m retaining the best project and documenting their strong correlation as a geometric—not phase-composition—uncertainty.

All requested deliverables now exist. I’m doing the final reproducibility audit: reload `final.gpx`, verify `fit.csv` is exactly the stored GSAS-II profile and statistics, parse the CIF independently, and confirm every JSON path/value resolves.

Completed. The modeled crystalline material is wolframite MnWO₄, P2/c (No. 13), at 100 wt% normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Final \(R_{wp}=3.7526\%\), GOF = 1.0878. No secondary crystalline phase was resolved reliably; amorphous content remains undetermined.

